=== HINT 1: fork ===
pid_t child = fork();

=== HINT 2: Child side ===
ptrace(PTRACE_TRACEME, 0, NULL, NULL);
raise(SIGSTOP);  // deliver SIGSTOP to self - parent's waitpid() wakes up

=== HINT 3: Parent - wait for stop ===
int status;
waitpid(child, &status, 0);
// WIFSTOPPED(status) && WSTOPSIG(status) == SIGSTOP

=== HINT 4: Read registers ===
struct user_regs_struct regs;
ptrace(PTRACE_GETREGS, child, NULL, &regs);
printf("RIP: 0x%llx  RSP: 0x%llx\n", regs.rip, regs.rsp);

=== HINT 5: Continue and wait ===
ptrace(PTRACE_CONT, child, NULL, NULL);
waitpid(child, &status, 0);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
ptrace is the foundation of: GDB, strace, ltrace, valgrind, QEMU user-mode.
PTRACE_SYSCALL stops the tracee on each syscall entry AND exit (2 stops per call).
PTRACE_SINGLESTEP executes one instruction then stops (SIGTRAP).
/proc/<pid>/mem + PTRACE_ATTACH = read/write any address without a signal.
Security: ptrace_scope (Yama LSM) default=1 restricts to parent→child ptrace.
